          "heading": "Lesson 3.1: Sensors and instrument response",
          "content": "**Learning goals.** Explain the mechanism, translate the problem into measurable quantities, test an alternative interpretation and defend an uncertainty statement.\n\n**Physical foundation.** Velocity sensors, accelerometers and broadband systems convert motion into signals with frequency-dependent gain and phase. Digitized counts require instrument metadata to be expressed as physical velocity, displacement or acceleration. In the broader unit, Reliable seismology starts with traceable measurement. Digitized amplitudes, timing systems and instrument calibration are not interchangeable; processing decisions alter the observability of phases and amplitudes. Archive station metadata with the actual waveform interval rather than assuming today\u2019s response applied historically.\n\n**Observational method.** Compare response-corrected and raw synthetic traces; record the poles, zeros, calibration factor and orientation metadata used. Specify the coordinate frame, observation period, relevant units, acquisition and calibration limitations before interpreting the resulting figure.\n\n**Common interpretive trap.** The tallest digital count is not necessarily the strongest actual motion when instruments or gains differ. A neat numeric solution does not remove this interpretive ambiguity. Ask which further independent measurement could reveal whether the preferred explanation is incorrect.\n\n**Applied investigation.** Document a corrected waveform with units and a record of the response-removal procedure. Provide a labeled sketch, a short reproducible table and a paragraph separating observed evidence from inferred process.\n\n#### Interpretation and quantitative example\n\n**Worked case 3.1: Nyquist frequency.** Synthetic input: `66.4` samples/s. Apply the explicitly declared relationship `x/2` to obtain **33.2 Hz**. Restrictions: Useful bandwidth also depends on analog antialias filters and sensor response. For this worked exercise, treat the input as a declared hypothetical value rather than a measurement. Copy the formula into a fresh calculation and check the unit conversion independently. Vary the input by ten percent and decide whether the output changes proportionally or nonlinearly. Explain which real-world conditions violate the assumptions listed. If the numeric result seems implausibly precise, round it appropriately and describe a physically meaningful range instead of inventing calibration. The purpose is transparent reasoning, not an official estimate. Trace all signal processing decisions to an observable consequence. If the spectrum changes after response removal, show the original count spectrum alongside the corrected SI spectrum. If a pick moves after filtering, report both times. If the sensor clipped, do not label amplitude restoration as a measurement unless an independent sensor constrains it.\n\n#### Evidence design and falsification\n\nReconstruct the inference backwards. Start with the headline result and locate every conversion, classification and assumption required to obtain it. An estimate based on a chosen velocity, phase pick or site parameter should be labelled conditional on those inputs. Review the same steps after making one intentional mistake in the input metadata; document which output would become misleading and why. Here, the principal source of ambiguity is stated explicitly: The tallest digital count is not necessarily the strongest actual motion when instruments or gains differ. Method A: compute a baseline result using the stated relation, preserving all units. Method B: perturb one relevant input and record the change. Method C: replace the simplest assumption with an alternative that would be plausible at the study scale; if you cannot recalculate it with the available information, state what is missing. Method D: compare with an independent kind of observation, if available. This order avoids hiding assumptions under additional significant figures. End the notebook by explaining how the proposed investigation (Document a corrected waveform with units and a record of the response-removal procedure.) addresses at least one weakness of the primary model.\n\n**Knowledge check.** (1) Which claim is directly observed and which is inferred? (2) How would an alternate model change the expected observation? (3) Which number must be accompanied by uncertainty? (4) Why is the worked result unsuitable as a real-time safety conclusion?",
