          "content": "**Learning goals.** Explain the mechanism, translate the problem into measurable quantities, test an alternative interpretation and defend an uncertainty statement.\n\n**Physical foundation.** Sample interval defines the Nyquist frequency, one half the sampling rate. Frequencies above it may alias unless appropriately filtered before sampling, and a high sample rate does not repair a noisy sensor. In the broader unit, Reliable seismology starts with traceable measurement. Digitized amplitudes, timing systems and instrument calibration are not interchangeable; processing decisions alter the observability of phases and amplitudes. Archive station metadata with the actual waveform interval rather than assuming today\u2019s response applied historically.\n\n**Observational method.** Generate two analytic sinusoids sampled at different rates and compare their discrete waveforms and apparent peaks. Specify the coordinate frame, observation period, relevant units, acquisition and calibration limitations before interpreting the resulting figure.\n\n**Common interpretive trap.** Interpolating an undersampled waveform can make a smooth but wrong picture; it cannot recover unknown lost frequencies. A neat numeric solution does not remove this interpretive ambiguity. Ask which further independent measurement could reveal whether the preferred explanation is incorrect.\n\n**Applied investigation.** Choose sampling and anti-alias constraints for distinct local and teleseismic signals. Provide a labeled sketch, a short reproducible table and a paragraph separating observed evidence from inferred process.\n\n#### Interpretation and quantitative example\n\n**Worked case 3.2: Illustrative observed alias of a 16 Hz tone.** Synthetic input: `20.16` samples/s. Apply the explicitly declared relationship `Math.abs(16-Math.round(16/x)*x)` to obtain **4.16 Hz**. Restrictions: Sampling ideal single sinusoid; analog anti-alias filters are deliberately omitted. For this worked exercise, treat the input as a declared hypothetical value rather than a measurement. Copy the formula into a fresh calculation and check the unit conversion independently. Vary the input by ten percent and decide whether the output changes proportionally or nonlinearly. Explain which real-world conditions violate the assumptions listed. If the numeric result seems implausibly precise, round it appropriately and describe a physically meaningful range instead of inventing calibration. The purpose is transparent reasoning, not an official estimate. Trace all signal processing decisions to an observable consequence. If the spectrum changes after response removal, show the original count spectrum alongside the corrected SI spectrum. If a pick moves after filtering, report both times. If the sensor clipped, do not label amplitude restoration as a measurement unless an independent sensor constrains it.\n\n#### Evidence design and falsification\n\nA strong evidence chain identifies what the observer physically controls. Specify waveform or map provenance, the acquisition time standard, the instrument or field method, the adopted calibration, and an independent comparison. Is the observed pattern repeatable across genuinely separate records or did it arise after multiple views of the same data? Where an observation is transformed into a calculated quantity, retain its original units and values. Write an audit note on which data would be decisive and which are merely compatible. Here, the principal source of ambiguity is stated explicitly: Interpolating an undersampled waveform can make a smooth but wrong picture; it cannot recover unknown lost frequencies. Create a four-row evidence matrix: observed quantity, inferred physical quantity, principal confounder, and proposed falsifying observation. Mark an entry as measured only when acquisition metadata is known. Repeat the matrix for a second hypothesis instead of dismissing it without calculation. This discipline prevents a tidy narrative from substituting for testable physical interpretation. End the notebook by explaining how the proposed investigation (Choose sampling and anti-alias constraints for distinct local and teleseismic signals.) addresses at least one weakness of the primary model.\n\n**Knowledge check.** (1) Which claim is directly observed and which is inferred? (2) How would an alternate model change the expected observation? (3) Which number must be accompanied by uncertainty? (4) Why is the worked result unsuitable as a real-time safety conclusion?",
