          "content": "**Research scenario:** A fictional coastal municipality, regional warning network and research group exchange maps, public reports and synthetic waveforms. Students assess separate roles of scientific analysis and authorized emergency institutions. The goal is to produce a reasoned comparison of at least two defensible models, not to reverse-engineer a predetermined answer. Start by recording each input variable with units, a confidence grade and a plausible range. Name the forward calculation associated with each inference and outline how the inputs enter the formula. Preserve the fabricated dataset in its supplied CSV form and cite its generation method; these records do not correspond to real earthquakes or real sensor networks.\n\n**Procedure.** First, draw a map or schematic that makes geometric assumptions visible. Second, check station or sample identifiers and the relationship of the observation period to the question. Third, compute baseline statistics and at least one worked relation by hand before using the browser model. Fourth, investigate a competing hypothesis using an independently chosen measurement or a perturbation of a consequential parameter. Fifth, repeat after withholding one doubtful observation. Compare whether the scientific conclusion survives that choice. A public-facing conclusion needs the responsible source, timestamp and boundaries. Distinguish modelled shaking from measured shaking, magnitude from impact, and a prospective alert from a retrospective interpretation. The fact that a student model renders consistently in a browser does not establish operational reliability or public authority.\n\n**Required outputs.** Submit a two-page technical brief with a hypothesis table, raw and corrected values, an equation inventory, an uncertainty budget, a labelled graphic and a short limitations section. Include all model parameters. Do not omit unsuccessful tests and do not represent computer-generated data as measured field observations. Students should record their own explanations rather than copying one another's interpretation. Discuss how different station distributions or sampling choices could cause an apparently significant spatial pattern without a corresponding physical change.\n\n**Extensions.** Seismologists distinguish what is observed, what is inferred and what decisions belong to public agencies or licensed engineers. The classroom models supplied here do not forecast earthquakes, calculate real building safety or authorize public alerts. Write an explicit counterfactual: what observation would undermine the main hypothesis? Consider what a larger dataset could resolve and what it would leave uncertain. Finally, design an accessible presentation of the result with a descriptive figure caption, a plain-language explanation of each symbol, and a table of numerical values for readers who cannot interpret a colored graph. Re-evaluate the result against professional source and public-safety boundaries before suggesting a consequential application.\n\n**Peer-review rubric.** Assign 0\u20134 points each for correctness of units, explicit assumptions, data provenance, comparison with alternatives, uncertainty treatment, computational reproducibility and ethical communication. A polished figure without units, or a convincing narrative without a falsifiable test, should not receive full credit. Explain in prose why the least certain input affects confidence, and identify at least one result that could change with additional observations.",
